<section class="page-head"><div class="container"><span class="status-pill completed">Completed</span><div class="eyebrow">Applied ML · Ranking · Search Intelligence</div><h1>FlyRank Applied Search Intelligence</h1><p class="section-lead"><strong>Which Content Pages Should Be Reviewed First?</strong> — A leakage-safe framework for future performance risk and human review.</p></div></section>
<section><div class="container content">
<section class="story-section"><h2>Project at a glance</h2><div class="story-grid"><div class="story-step"><strong>1 · Objective</strong><p>Identify which content pages are most worth reviewing when search performance may decline and human review capacity is limited.</p></div><div class="story-step"><strong>2 · Data</strong><p>Decision-time search-performance data from a large pseudonymised warehouse release; the final balanced proof-of-concept used 2,520 pages from 21 clients.</p></div><div class="story-step"><strong>3 · Method</strong><p>Combine classification and signed regression signals into a top-50 review ranking using only information available before the April outcome period.</p></div><div class="story-step"><strong>4 · Evaluation</strong><p>Use client-separated grouped validation and a six-client unseen stress test against transparent frozen baselines.</p></div><div class="story-step story-impact"><strong>5 · Outcome</strong><p>Development validation improved over baseline, but classification and ranking did not generalise above baseline on the unseen-client stress test; that limitation is retained in the final conclusion.</p></div></div></section>
<h2>Context and objective</h2>
<p>The capstone asks which content pages should be reviewed first. It uses decision-time information from March 2026 to estimate April performance change and prioritise limited human review capacity. The goal is directional decision support, not a claim to predict Google's algorithm.</p>

<h2>Data and constraints</h2>
<p>The public workflow uses anonymised data and avoids raw private client identifiers, domains, URLs, titles and keywords. The final proof-of-concept uses a balanced lane of <strong>2,520 pages across 21 clients</strong>, with six clients reserved for the final unseen stress test.</p>

<h2>Technical solution</h2>
<ul>
<li>Use only features available at the decision date to prevent future-information leakage.</li>
<li>Frame the problem across classification, signed regression and ranking rather than forcing one metric to answer every business question.</li>
<li>Combine classification and signed regression into a top-50 human-review ranking.</li>
<li>Keep transparent frozen baselines as the minimum standard the learned models must beat.</li>
</ul>
<div class="callout">The ranking prioritises attention; the final content decision remains with a person.</div>

<h2>Evaluation and evidence</h2>
<p>Clients are separated during evaluation so validation measures transfer rather than memorisation of client-specific patterns. Development validation is followed by a six-client stress test that deliberately asks whether the learned signals generalise to completely unseen clients.</p>

<h2>Key results</h2>
<p>Development validation improved over the frozen baselines. On the six-client stress test, regression improved modestly, while <strong>classification and ranking did not improve over their transparent baselines</strong>. That mixed result is retained rather than hidden.</p>

<h2>Limitations and scope</h2>
<p>The work provides observed, measured, directional decision support. It does not claim to model Google's algorithm or guarantee future search outcomes. The unseen-client result shows that the strongest development patterns did not transfer reliably enough for unrestricted automated decision making.</p>

<h2>Practical relevance</h2>
<p>The system is designed to help a content team focus scarce review time on pages that may warrant attention first, while keeping human review as the final decision layer.</p>

<h2>What this demonstrates</h2>
<p>Leakage-safe ML design, transparent baselines, grouped transfer evaluation, combination of multiple prediction tasks into an operational ranking and honest reporting of weaker out-of-sample generalisation.</p>

